-- D6 state foundation, tier C continued: Public Service/Utility
-- Commissioner, the 10 states where it's a popularly ELECTED multi-member
-- commission (of 50 -- exists everywhere, appointed by the governor in
-- 38 of the other 40, by a non-gubernatorial method in Virginia and
-- South Carolina). Same shape as every migration in this tier -- small
-- hand-verified data, not an ingester.
--
-- Deliberately deferred from migration 065 because this office is
-- structurally different from every single-person office built so far:
-- multi-member (3 to 7 seats), with THREE different real seat-
-- organization patterns across the 10 elected states, confirmed by
-- fetching each state's own PSC page directly (Selection Method + Total
-- Seats + Length of term fields), not inferred from the aggregate
-- officeholder-list table alone:
--  - District-numbered (one seat per numbered district, modeled as
--    single-seat offices, same pattern as this project's state-
--    legislature and county-council-district work): Georgia (5, 6yr),
--    Louisiana (5, 6yr), Montana (5, 4yr), Nebraska (5, 6yr), and
--    Mississippi (3 NAMED, not numbered, districts -- Central/Northern/
--    Southern, 4yr).
--  - At-large multi-seat (all members elected statewide, no individual
--    seat identifier -- one office, seat_count = N, same multi-seat-pool
--    pattern this schema already uses for Montgomery County's at-large
--    council seats): Arizona (5, 4yr), North Dakota (3, 6yr), Oklahoma
--    (3, 6yr), South Dakota (3, 6yr).
--  - Alabama: a real, still-in-progress structural transition (Code of
--    Alabama Title 37-1-1) from a 3-member at-large commission to a
--    7-member by-district commission, phased in through 2033. Only 3
--    seats currently exist and are filled (President -- itself a
--    directly-elected seat, not an internally-chosen chair -- plus
--    Place 1 and Place 2); Places 3-7 are not yet created, so NOT
--    modeled, same "doesn't exist yet, not merely vacant" discipline
--    already used elsewhere in this project for not-yet-created seats.
--    Term length is also transitioning, 4 years to 6 for commissioners
--    serving from June 1, 2026 onward -- this migration uses 6 (the
--    incoming constitutional standard) for the office row, though the
--    sitting President's own actual term began under the old 4-year
--    rule; disclosed in her bio text, not silently normalized away.
--
-- Real false positive caught and corrected before applying: a first-pass
-- party-affiliation heuristic (same technique already known to be
-- unreliable from migration 065's Insurance Commissioner catch) matched
-- 11 candidate states, not 10 -- Illinois's Commerce Commission carries
-- real party tags on its commissioners despite being "Appointed by the
-- governor" per its own Ballotpedia page's Selection Method field.
-- Excluded. Every one of the 10 states actually included here was
-- individually confirmed "Selection Method: Elected" on its own page,
-- not accepted on the heuristic alone -- same discipline as migration
-- 065 going forward after the Insurance Commissioner and Superintendent
-- catches showed the heuristic alone isn't trustworthy for this whole
-- category of office.
--
-- A few source rows gave only a bare year for very long-tenured
-- incumbents (Oklahoma's Todd Hiett: "2015"; South Dakota's Gary Hanson:
-- "2003"; Nebraska's Tim Schram: "2007") -- term_start for those defaults
-- to January 1 of that year, flagged in each row's own bio text.



